  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Broad area of high pressure in place across the \nSoutheast U.S. will gradually expand eastward and offshore. This \nwill allow low level winds to veer from the north to northeast \ntoday, with wind speeds increasing to 5-10 mph this afternoon. \nAirmass will remain rather dry, but the onshore winds and an \nincrease in low level moisture may generate isolated showers and \nsprinkles over the waters that are able to push onshore. Hi-res and \nglobal model guidance keeps this activity mostly near to north of \nthe Cape today, with a slight (20%) chance for onshore moving \nshowers along much of the coast into tonight. Otherwise, mostly dry \nconditions are forecast to continue for much of the area. \n\nTemperatures will start off pleasant in the mid to upper 60s, with \nhighs near to just a tad above normal today in the mid to upper 80s. \nAs onshore flow becomes more established across the area, min temps \ntonight are forecast to remain in the low to mid 70s along much of \nthe coast, with upper 60s to low 70s forecast inland. \n  \nWednesday-Thursday...High pressure at the surface remains north of \nthe area, keeping winds onshore through midweek. East-northeast \nwinds will increase up to 10-15 mph each afternoon (strongest along \nthe coast). Isolated showers pushing onshore and developing along a \ndiffuse inland moving east coast sea breeze will be possible \ntomorrow, with rain chances around 20 percent mainly southeast of \nthe I-4 corridor. Moisture and instability will then continue to \ngradually increase across the area Thursday. Rain chances rise to 20-\n50% near to south of Orlando, with a few storms possible into the \nthe afternoon. Mid level ridge across the west Atlantic extending \nacross the area will keep temps aloft rather warm (around -5 to \n-6C at 500 mb), so this should keep any stronger storm threat low. \n\nTemperatures remain near to slightly above normal, with highs in the \nmid to upper 80s. Onshore winds will keep overnight lows in the \nlow to mid 70s for most locations. \n\nFriday-Monday...High pressure ridge and onshore flow weakens Friday \nas a cold front sweeps through the southeast U.S. and eventually \nacross the area into Saturday. Winds briefly become northerly as \nfront moves through, but strong area of high pressure that builds in \nwell north of the front will quickly turn winds onshore into the \nweekend. A tightening pressure gradient between the front and ridge \nto the north, will lead to breezy to windy conditions developing \nbehind this boundary as early as Saturday and likely persisting \nthrough early next week. Rain chances remain near to above normal \nthrough the period, with scattered to numerous showers and isolated \nto scattered storms each day. This activity will mostly be limited \nto the afternoon and early evening hours on Friday. However, into \nthe weekend and through Monday deep moisture combined with strong \nlow level onshore flow will continue rounds of gusty showers and \nstorms pushing onshore. Locally heavy to excessive rainfall could \nalso be possible, mainly along the coast where any persistent \nbands of showers and storms can develop. \n\nThe longer duration windy east-northeast flow will also lead to to \nthe development of dangerous surf and boating conditions, and will \nalso increase the potential for beach erosion this weekend into \nearly next week. Max temps will still reach the mid to upper 80s on \nFriday, but are forecast to trend slightly cooler behind the front, \nfalling to the low to mid 80s Saturday and then upper 70s to low 80s \non Sunday. Overnight lows will remain near to above normal as gusty \nonshore flow continues.",
